**Merge-blocking: the runner's new end-of-run strip takes the turn labels off nodes that are still open — after every run that hands back to a person, the node ends wearing neither label.**

`applyTurnLabels` short-circuits `phase === "closed"` and never consults the node's state for that phase: `src/entrypoints/machinery/lib/turn-labels.ts:79` guards the state read with `if (phase !== "closed")`, so execution falls straight into `remove(waitingForPerson); remove(inProgress)` at `src/entrypoints/machinery/lib/turn-labels.ts:88-91`, unconditionally on the state, for whatever that phase is given. The new runner step passes exactly that phase and is unconditional: `src/workflows/atomaton-runner.wac.ts:1628-1638` is `if: "always()"` with `--phase "closed"`, and `src/workflows/atomaton-runner.wac.ts:2370-2371` places it immediately after the release step (`:1598-1617`) that has just added `waiting-for-person`. So a normal run end does: release removes `in-progress` and adds `waiting-for-person` → strip reads the open node, sees `waiting-for-person` on it, and removes it. Same step on a `handed-off`/`parked` ending (which skip the release by design) removes the `in-progress` guard the park is holding. This contradicts the step's own comment ("on an open node the script reads the state, finds the phase still true, and writes nothing") and the documentation this PR adds.

**Correction required:** make the `closed` phase read the state the way the other two do, and write nothing when it reads `open` — e.g. for `phase === "closed"`, return before the remove branch when `state.known && state.state === "open"`, and strip only when the read says closed/merged (keeping today's unconditional removal when the read fails). Add the test that is missing: `run("closed", [stateOpen, ...])` must produce no `--remove-label`, no `--add-label` and no `label create` — the mirror of `src/entrypoints/machinery/manage_turn_labels.test.ts:80`, which today only exercises phase `closed` against a closed node.

<details><summary>Evidence: the phase switch, the step, and the order it runs in</summary>

- `src/entrypoints/machinery/lib/turn-labels.ts:78-92`
  ```
  let effective = phase;
  if (phase !== "closed") { ...bend on state... }
  if (effective === "closed") {
    remove(waitingForPerson);
    remove(inProgress);
    return;
  }
  ```
  `remove` is presence-aware (`:68-72`), so on an open node wearing `waiting-for-person` it issues the `--remove-label`.
- `src/workflows/atomaton-runner.wac.ts:1630` — `if: "always()"`; `:1636` — `scriptCommandWithArgs(manageTurnLabelsRef, { phase: "closed", number: "\${NUMBER}" })`.
- `src/workflows/atomaton-runner.wac.ts:2370-2371` — `removeLabelStep` then `stripClosedLabelsStep`, in that order, in one job's step list.
- `tests/contract/turn-labels.test.ts:38-45` pins only that the strip step exists and is `always()` — it cannot see the effect, which is why CI stays green.

The rest of the coverage the issue asked for is right: a person's PR close/merge hits `strip-turn-labels` in `src/workflows/atomaton-pr-closed.wac.ts:311-334`, a person's issue close hits it in `src/workflows/atomaton-sub-issue-closed.wac.ts:108-120,193-200` (that workflow fires on every `issues: closed`, not only sub-issues), and an agent's own close is the runner strip — both jobs have checkout, bun, `GH_TOKEN`, the number from the event/inputs, and `issues: write` from `ATOMATON_WORKFLOW_PERMISSIONS` (`src/workflows/actions/permissions.ts:16-29`). No code reads either label to decide anything, so removing them on close cannot strand a guard (`running` comes from the thread, `src/adapters/github/work-tree.ts:85-101`; the only other reader is pruning).

